course: dictionary
lesson: 12683
title: Disturbances: noise or riots?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one. «Disturbances» is a good example. Most of the time it means things that interrupt you, like noise or trouble. «The loud drilling was a disturbance that stopped our reading.» Here the drilling broke our calm.
explain: News reports and officials sometimes use «disturbances» in a different way. «The police said there were disturbances in the town centre last night.» Here it means riots, fights or violent protests, but the word sounds calmer than what really happened.
explain: This is called a euphemism: a softer word used in place of a harsher one. To find the meaning, read the clues around it. Words like police, violence or streets point to the serious meaning, while words like drilling or interrupted point to the everyday meaning.
fact: «Disturbances» means things that disturb or interrupt, such as noise or trouble.
fact: In news and official reports, «disturbances» can mean riots, fights or violent protests.
fact: This use is a euphemism: a calmer word that softens something more serious.
fact: «Disturbances» is mainly used in the UK, especially in official language.
fact: «Disturbances» is the plural of «disturbance», so it means more than one.
exercise: «The loud drilling was a disturbance that stopped our reading.» What does disturbance mean here? | *Noise that interrupts | A quiet, peaceful rest | A gift from a friend | A kind word
exercise: The news said: «There were disturbances in the town centre last night, with police on the streets.» What does disturbances mean here? | Happy street parties | *Riots and violent protests | Lost property at the station | Sunny weather for the market
exercise: Find and copy the word that means things that interrupt: «Two phone calls were disturbances during our quiet lesson.» | lesson | *disturbances | phone | quiet
test: Why might a news report say «disturbances» instead of «riots»? | *It sounds calmer, which is how official language often softens serious events | It means happy crowds at a festival | It is a word for heavy rain | It means very loud music from a band
test: «Heavy rain and a broken bus were disturbances to our trip.» What does disturbances mean here? | Sunny skies all day | *Interruptions and troubles | Free tickets for the zoo | Party games
exam: «The police said the disturbances were over by morning.» Which meaning fits best? | Long queues at the shops | Lost children found safely | *Riots and fights in the streets | Quiet music in the park
